 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on",
   "text": "Niemann-Pick C is caused by changes in a gene called NPC1, which makes a protein that normally sits in a part of the cell called a late endosome [Ed1828b2]. Doctors confirm the diagnosis by finding two changed copies of the NPC1 gene, or sometimes the related NPC2 gene, through genetic testing [Ec1263ea]. When NPC1 does not work properly, cells have trouble moving a fat called cholesterol to where it is needed, so cholesterol builds up inside the cell [E0214ff1, Ec87692e]. Along with cholesterol, other fatty substances called glycolipids build up too [E4ed5539]. This buildup happens inside parts of the cell called lysosomes, which normally act like the cell's recycling centers, and these recycling centers stop working the way they should [Ebea9f4a]. Cells can also show stress in another cell part called the ER (short for endoplasmic reticulum, a part of the cell that helps build proteins), on top of the lysosome problems [Ebecfcc9]. Over time, this is linked with the loss of nerve cells in the brain, which doctors call neurodegeneration [E7ef7319, Ebf1e732].",
