  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "What exists already",
   "text": "The Australian NPC Disease Foundation Inc supports people and families affected by Niemann-Pick disease type C in Australia [E794c61a]. The International Niemann-Pick Disease Alliance (INPDA), based in the United Kingdom, is a global network of groups supporting people with Niemann-Pick diseases [Eaff0524]. The National Niemann-Pick Disease Foundation, Inc. (NNPDF), based in the United States, supports patients and families affected by Niemann-Pick disease [E09def7b]. A separate organization, National Niemann-Pick disease Foundation, Inc., also based in the United States, offers similar support [Ebbaa3ef]. Niemann-Pick UK (NPUK) supports individuals, families, and friends affected by Niemann-Pick diseases [E8f89460]. A study called 'Evaluation of Biochemical Markers and Clinical Investigation of Niemann-Pick Disease, Type C' is recruiting participants in the United States, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E79bd406, E2bf1fc8]. A Phase 3 study is testing an oral medicine called Nizubaglustat, also known as AZ-3102, against a placebo in people with late-infantile and juvenile Niemann-Pick disease type C, and is recruiting in countries including the United States, Canada, Brazil, and Australia, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E9cf59f8, E494d62f, Efedd728, Eddb75f2, E50a9323, E4fe4594, E98cecb4]. Another Phase 3 study of the same medicine includes people with Niemann-Pick disease type C together with GM1 gangliosidosis and GM2 gangliosidosis, and is recruiting across many countries, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E37ca5ed, E05ee7b4, E8856918]. A completed study used brain-monitoring methods called fNIRS and DCS to look at neurocognitive disease compared with healthy volunteers in the United States, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E060d92d, Ebfb87ac]. NIH NINDS funds research on NPC1 protein misfolding, led by researchers Andrew P Lieberman and Brian S J Blagg, according to NIH RePORTER [E4f395f9, Eab56acf, Ed6df02f, E9acea00]. NIH NINDS also funds research on a cell process called ferroptosis in Niemann-Pick disease type C, led by researcher Pamela Anne Maher, according to NIH RePORTER [Eecbcff9, E3cf86a2, Ed1aa9be]. NIH NINDS funds research on lysosome-related mechanisms of nerve cell loss connected to Niemann-Pick disease type C, led by researcher Roberto Zoncu, according to NIH RePORTER [E301564c, Ef6a6369, E024fce5]. Schuchman, according to NIH RePORTER [E59fb599, Ed51b44e, E739dfaf]. NIH NIGMS funds research connected to Niemann-Pick disease type C on how lysosomes control connections to the cell's outer membrane, led by researcher Eamonn James Dickson, according to NIH RePORTER [E245c595, E3e46e7f, E96b9939].",
